course: dictionary
lesson: 8416
title: Runny: when things flow easily
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Runny means it has more liquid than usual, so it flows easily. «Runny honey» pours off the spoon, and a «runny egg» has a yolk that spreads out when you break it.
explain: A runny nose has a lot of liquid coming out of it, often from a cold or hay fever. The words around it give clues: «Hay fever ... causes sneezing, itching and a runny nose.» Sneezing and itching tell you the problem is in the nose.
explain: Scientists also use runny for liquids that flow easily, such as water, compared with thick, sticky ones. Always test your meaning: swap it in, and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Runny means flowing easily, with more liquid than usual.
fact: Runny can describe food, such as «runny custard», or your body, such as a «runny nose» or «runny eyes».
fact: Runny eyes are watery eyes.
fact: In science, runny is everyday language for a liquid with low viscosity, which flows easily, rather than thick or sticky.
fact: Thick and sticky are the opposites of runny in the sense of flowing.
exercise: Which of these is a runny food? | Solid ice cubes | Thick peanut butter | Dry bread crumbs | *Runny custard
exercise: Hay fever can cause «a runny nose». What does this mean? | The nose is very cold | The nose is blocked and hard | The nose smells of flowers | *A lot of liquid comes out of the nose
exercise: «The glowing runny glass is cooled into sheets, bottles and jars.» What does runny mean here? | Cold and dry | Hard like rock | Thick and lumpy | *Flowing like a liquid
test: «Basalt is made from runny lava.» What does runny tell you about the lava? | *Flowing easily, like a liquid | Thick and slow | Dark and cold | Solid like a stone
test: Mia says runny means sticky. Why is she wrong? | Runny means very hot | *Sticky is a synonym for runny | Runny means flowing easily, not sticky | Runny means dry
exam: «Their spit keeps the blood runny so it does not clot.» What does runny mean here? | Thick and clumpy | Red and warm | *Flowing easily, so it does not clot | Cold and dry
